WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — ai-reads-human (90s) Narrator Sean

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.127
First, computers often swap data in text formats written for human eyes,

2
00:00:05.127 --> 00:00:07.598
forcing machines to spend extra work.

3
00:00:08.098 --> 00:00:12.320
Second, reading indented configuration text took over two thousand

4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.158
times more chip energy than reading compact machine numbers.

5
00:00:16.658 --> 00:00:20.702
Third, chopping structured text into AI word pieces burned

6
00:00:20.702 --> 00:00:24.468
twelve times more energy than parsing the text itself.

7
00:00:24.968 --> 00:00:30.489
Formats full of curly braces, commas, and indented spaces were designed so people could

8
00:00:30.489 --> 00:00:36.074
inspect files comfortably in text editors. Yet modern AI tool pipelines pass millions of

9
00:00:36.074 --> 00:00:41.722
those verbose text records strictly from machine to machine without a human ever looking.

10
00:00:42.222 --> 00:00:46.964
We named this extra work the impedance tax and tested thirty-four parsing and

11
00:00:46.964 --> 00:00:53.615
word-splitting workloads across twenty thousand records. Running each pass inside an isolated worker process

12
00:00:53.615 --> 00:00:59.589
on an Apple processor cleanly separated tiny parsing costs from noisy background laptop activity.

13
00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:06.617
Unpacking a twenty-three-byte binary record used two hundred eighty nanojoules. Standard bracketed text

14
00:01:06.617 --> 00:01:12.067
used nearly eight times more, compiled indented text used two hundred forty-nine times

15
00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:17.771
more, and pure Python indented text burned two thousand fifty-four times more chip energy.

16
00:01:18.271 --> 00:01:26.048
Even adding two spaces of pretty indentation more than doubled AI word-splitting energy to seventy-one microjoules per record.

17
00:01:26.048 --> 00:01:31.355
Keep internal machine pipes compact and binary, and format text only when people read.
